A||||9||FIEROMJ||Fiero Trouble||||4||
Z||000000||FIEROMJ||04-02-2001||09:49 PM||||Hey, I'm new to the message board.  I own a white '87 GT w/ about 89,000 miles on it. Just recently it has developed a ticking sound and something else that sounds like a pop.  My oil pressure gage has never worked properly (the needle bounces around) but while driving I noticed my pressure light came on. My car has also lost acceleration in a major way.  I already checked the oil and it is full.  Recently I changed the oil, oil filter, air filter and TPS.  I have driven my car once since this happened and had to drive right back home cause it sounded horrible.  I am not receiving any "check engine" light either. Is it just the oil pump?  Thanks for reading, please help.||||reg||4
Z||000001||btoth||04-02-2001||09:55 PM||||The "pop" may be backfiring you're hearing.  A clogged catalytic converter will cause that and will dramatically effect acceleration as well as throw off sensors.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-02-2001||10:01 PM||||Just a shot in the dark, but the ticking makes me think maybe a collapsed or bad lifter, or other valvetrain problem. <BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||04-02-2001||10:07 PM||||O c'mon guys, its a friggen exhaust leak - not the car self destructing or getting ready to burn a huge hole in your wallet<P>causes - cracked exhaust manifold, unproperly tightened exhaust hardware, cracked y-tube, exhaust "doughnut" failure where y-tube meets lower exhaust, or leak from in or around the EGR valve location.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||04-02-2001||10:25 PM||||In what plane of existance does an exhaust leak cause substantial power loss? (unless it's the previously mentioned plugged convertor, which probably *caused* the leak.<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROMJ||04-02-2001||10:28 PM||||So, would an exhaust leak make it drive like a complete piece of crap?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark||04-02-2001||10:43 PM||||I believe the oil pressure sender also controls the fuel pump (i.e., no oil pressure = no fuel).  Since your oil pressure indication is erratic, you may be losing fuel pressure.  I would get a new oil pressure sender...under $20 if I remember correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000007||RNGCRSH||04-02-2001||10:47 PM||||I agree with the exaust leak theory. Mine developed an exhaust leak in the usual location. I hear ticking and when I accelerate I get a poping sound in the muffler (bakfires slightly when I let off gas). the engine runs rich but codes for lean F/A mix. If he has a large enough crack in the manifold he could have warped a valve causing the power loss.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JustBein'Michael||04-03-2001||12:12 AM||||Hey...I am new to this board. My Fiero hasn't done that yet...Well, because it doesn't really run right now(but that's a whole 'nuther story).  However, my Sportage just did.  Major loss of power.  Crappy sound.  Only 35mph top speed(25 on a slight incline--like highway on ramps).  Turned out to be a cat converter that was blocked(read as-all melted and rusted inside).  Once replaced, the mighty Kia went back up to the usual 55mph top end.  LOL.<P>JustBein'Michael||||reg||1
Z||000009||pontiacs4me||04-03-2001||02:29 AM||||An exhaust leak can cause a substantial power loss.  I dont know why, but my friend Andy used to have a 69 mustang, and the only time it ever ran good was when he had no exhaust leaks, I have a 73 bird, and it has a warped header flange, with a leak on the underside of the head, I lost 4 tenths of a second at the track because of it.  My friend Tom has a 91 lumina with a 3800 series II supercharged in it, he drove to St louis 2 weekends ago to a GTP race and ran a 13.3, came back here and last weekend ran a 13.6 because his leak got worse.  He lost 10 mph trap speed also.  I dont think differences in track conditions could cause that much of a variance.  I just know that the engines are designed to operate their best with no intake/exhaust/vacuum leaks, with all the sensors hooked up etc, etc.  BTW, when I first got my car running, I had a real bad egr leak, at the base of the egr valve.  I was shooting trouble code 32, and the car would backfire when I shut if off.  I put a new gasket in there and some muffler mend as a gasket sealant, and no more backfires, and no more trouble codes.||||reg||1
